1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,449
The invitation came in March, on paper, because the
people who sent it still believed

2
00:00:13,469 --> 00:00:14,049
in paper.

3
00:00:14,449 --> 00:00:20,641
The World Health and Industry Forum meets in Basel
every June: four thousand delegates,

4
00:00:20,661 --> 00:00:26,853
eleven heads of regulatory agencies, the chief
executives of every pharmaceutical house

5
00:00:26,873 --> 00:00:33,207
on earth, three health ministers, and a press pool of
six hundred. It is not a scientific

6
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:38,419
meeting. It is the room in which the industry
decides who is respectable.

7
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:44,683
They did not invite Umbrella to give a keynote. They
invited Umbrella to sit on a panel

8
00:00:44,703 --> 00:00:50,567
about equitable access, in the second session, on
the Tuesday, with four other speakers

9
00:00:50,587 --> 00:00:51,649
and a moderator.

10
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:56,858
It's an insult. Politely, elaborately, expensively
phrased — and an insult. We are the

11
00:00:56,878 --> 00:01:01,731
largest company in the industry by market
capitalisation and they have put you on a panel

12
00:01:01,751 --> 00:01:03,318
at twenty-one in the morning.

13
00:01:03,719 --> 00:01:04,341
Yes.

14
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:06,448
I have drafted a refusal.

15
00:01:06,849 --> 00:01:07,910
Accept it.

16
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:09,078
Albert —

17
00:01:09,478 --> 00:01:14,984
She had begun using his first name that winter,
which nobody else in the company did,

18
00:01:15,004 --> 00:01:17,649
and which he had never once acknowledged.

19
00:01:18,168 --> 00:01:22,719
If you take that chair you will be photographed in a
row of five, and next year they will

20
00:01:22,739 --> 00:01:27,495
offer you the same chair, and the year after that you
will still be the interesting newcomer.

21
00:01:27,515 --> 00:01:30,526
That room does not promote you. You
have to take the stage.

22
00:01:30,926 --> 00:01:33,416
I intend to. Accept the panel.

23
00:01:33,816 --> 00:01:40,215
He arrived in Basel the night before the conference
opened and did something he had not

24
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:44,715
done since a dark auditorium in
Wilmington two years earlier.

25
00:01:45,236 --> 00:01:46,346
I need you.

26
00:01:46,745 --> 00:01:50,536
Say that again. Slowly. I would
like to enjoy it.

27
00:01:50,936 --> 00:01:55,971
I have five minutes on a panel at eleven o'clock on
Tuesday and four thousand people in

28
00:01:55,991 --> 00:02:01,200
that hall who believe I am a diagnostics company that
got lucky with a longevity molecule.

29
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:06,429
I intend to leave Basel as the thing the industry is
organised around. You have done this.

30
00:02:06,449 --> 00:02:07,127
Tell me how.

31
00:02:07,527 --> 00:02:13,750
And Adolf — the passenger, the other voice, the thing
in his skull that was good at exactly

32
00:02:13,770 --> 00:02:19,582
one thing in all the world — was quiet for a long
moment, and then began to talk, and

33
00:02:19,602 --> 00:02:21,228
he talked for two hours.

34
00:02:21,628 --> 00:02:27,476
The narrator will give you the shape of it, because
it is the only extended thing this

35
00:02:27,496 --> 00:02:33,343
voice ever says, and because everything Albert
Wesker does in front of a crowd for the

36
00:02:33,363 --> 00:02:35,798
next sixty years is built out of it.

37
00:02:36,318 --> 00:02:38,915
First: you will not use the five minutes.

38
00:02:39,316 --> 00:02:41,075
I have five minutes.

39
00:02:41,475 --> 00:02:46,118
You have five minutes in which four other people are
also speaking, and the room is at

40
00:02:46,138 --> 00:02:50,727
half attention, and the man before you will
overrun. Every one of those four will use

41
00:02:50,747 --> 00:02:55,443
their time. You will use ninety seconds of yours,
and you will say something that makes

42
00:02:55,463 --> 00:03:00,160
the other four sound like children, and then you
will stop, and the silence will belong

43
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:04,769
to you. A crowd does not remember who spoke
longest. It remembers who was still there

44
00:03:04,789 --> 00:03:06,233
after the speaking stopped.

45
00:03:06,513 --> 00:03:12,570
Second: you must give them something to be afraid of
that is not you. They came to Basel

46
00:03:12,590 --> 00:03:18,648
to talk about access and pricing and supply. That is
a room full of people arguing about

47
00:03:18,668 --> 00:03:24,519
how to divide a thing. Do not argue. Tell them what
is coming for all of them, and be

48
00:03:24,539 --> 00:03:28,594
the only person in the building who is
not surprised by it.

49
00:03:28,875 --> 00:03:33,914
Third — and you will dislike this one — you must
give something away. Not a discount.

50
00:03:33,934 --> 00:03:39,151
Something that costs you visibly, in public, that no
one asked for and no one can match,

51
00:03:39,171 --> 00:03:44,806
and it must be announced in a flat voice as though it
were an administrative detail. Generosity

52
00:03:44,826 --> 00:03:50,222
delivered as a *decision* is the most frightening
thing a powerful man can perform, because

53
00:03:50,242 --> 00:03:52,543
it proves the power was never in doubt.

54
00:03:52,943 --> 00:03:54,053
And the pause.

55
00:03:54,454 --> 00:04:00,068
The pause is everything. When you have given it to
them, do not explain it. Do not soften

56
00:04:00,088 --> 00:04:05,449
it. Stand still and let four thousand people
discover what they think while you watch

57
00:04:05,469 --> 00:04:11,019
them do it. You will want to fill that silence. That
wanting is the thing you must kill.

58
00:04:11,419 --> 00:04:17,677
The panel ran for fifty-one minutes. The man before
him — the chief executive of a Swiss

59
00:04:17,697 --> 00:04:22,029
house four times Umbrella's age — spoke
for nineteen of them.

60
00:04:22,428 --> 00:04:28,438
When the moderator finally turned and said, with the
particular brightness of a person

61
00:04:28,458 --> 00:04:34,538
conscious of the clock, *"Dr. Wesker, perhaps you
could give us Umbrella's perspective,

62
00:04:34,558 --> 00:04:41,059
briefly,"* Albert Wesker leaned forward to the
microphone and did not begin for four seconds.

63
00:04:41,908 --> 00:04:46,945
There will be a pandemic in the next nineteen years that
our industry, as currently organised,

64
00:04:46,965 --> 00:04:47,699
cannot answer.

65
00:04:48,418 --> 00:04:52,859
Not because the science will be beyond us. The science
was not beyond us last time. Because

66
00:04:52,879 --> 00:04:57,026
the answer will exist in three laboratories, and it
will take nine months to license,

67
00:04:57,046 --> 00:05:01,193
four months to manufacture, and twenty-two weeks to
distribute, and the people it was

68
00:05:01,213 --> 00:05:05,408
for will have died in the first twenty-two weeks.
That is not a scientific failure. It

69
00:05:05,428 --> 00:05:09,575
is an administrative one, and every person on this
stage, including me, is paid to be

70
00:05:09,595 --> 00:05:10,409
an administrator.

71
00:05:10,809 --> 00:05:16,753
The hall had gone very quiet, in the way that four
thousand people go quiet: unevenly,

72
00:05:16,773 --> 00:05:18,626
in patches, from the front.

73
00:05:19,146 --> 00:05:21,752
I am not going to propose a working group.

74
00:05:22,031 --> 00:05:26,432
As of this morning, Umbrella's rapid assay
platform, the whole of it — the instrument

75
00:05:26,452 --> 00:05:31,112
specification, the cartridge design, the reagent
formulations, the two-stream lysis patent

76
00:05:31,132 --> 00:05:35,637
and every improvement we have made to it in three
years — is placed in an open licence.

77
00:05:35,657 --> 00:05:40,577
Free. Permanently. To any manufacturer in any country,
including the four companies represented

78
00:05:40,597 --> 00:05:45,257
on this stage, on the single condition that the
product is sold at or below eleven dollars

79
00:05:45,277 --> 00:05:45,621
a test.

80
00:05:46,451 --> 00:05:51,430
Our filing went in at nine o'clock this morning. It
is irrevocable. Our counsel advised

81
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:52,062
against it.

82
00:05:52,461 --> 00:05:58,244
The narrator would like to record what that decision
actually cost, because the room did

83
00:05:58,264 --> 00:06:03,915
not know and Wesker had made certain the number was
in the filing where any journalist

84
00:06:03,935 --> 00:06:09,520
could find it by lunchtime: four hundred and eleven
million dollars a year in licence

85
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:15,322
revenue, given away, permanently, in a sentence, by a
man who did not appear to consider

86
00:06:15,342 --> 00:06:16,312
it interesting.

87
00:06:16,831 --> 00:06:22,299
I have been asked to speak about equitable access. That
is what I have to say about equitable

88
00:06:22,319 --> 00:06:23,362
access. Thank you.

89
00:06:23,762 --> 00:06:27,581
And then he sat back from the microphone
and said nothing at all.

90
00:06:28,641 --> 00:06:34,119
The moderator, who had eight minutes of programme
left and a question prepared for the

91
00:06:34,139 --> 00:06:39,808
next speaker, looked down at her card. The next
speaker did not reach for his microphone.

92
00:06:39,828 --> 00:06:45,817
Somewhere in the middle of the hall a single person
began to applaud and stopped, embarrassed,

93
00:06:45,837 --> 00:06:47,032
when nobody joined.

94
00:06:47,432 --> 00:06:52,002
The silence lasted, by the conference's own
recording, nine seconds.

95
00:06:52,401 --> 00:06:53,831
Then the hall stood up.

96
00:06:54,232 --> 00:06:59,665
Not everyone. The chief executive of the Swiss house
remained seated, and so did two of

97
00:06:59,685 --> 00:07:05,055
the regulators, who understood precisely what had
just been done to their industry and

98
00:07:05,075 --> 00:07:10,571
who were the only people in the building fully awake.
But four thousand people in a hall

99
00:07:10,591 --> 00:07:15,899
stand the way water finds a level, and inside
twenty-nine seconds the room was on its

100
00:07:15,919 --> 00:07:20,600
feet applauding a man who had spoken for one
hundred and forty-one seconds.

101
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:27,125
Albert Wesker stood up, nodded once, and left the
stage while they were still clapping.

102
00:07:27,145 --> 00:07:30,940
He did not do a press line. He did
not take questions.

103
00:07:31,339 --> 00:07:35,550
He was in a car on the way to the airport
before the session ended.

104
00:07:35,949 --> 00:07:42,661
There is one more piece of the Basel preparation and
the narrator has held it back, because

105
00:07:42,681 --> 00:07:49,319
it is the only time the passenger in Albert Wesker's
skull asks for something for himself.

106
00:07:49,839 --> 00:07:54,738
When you go out there tomorrow. There will be
four thousand people in that hall.

107
00:07:55,139 --> 00:07:55,761
Yes.

108
00:07:56,161 --> 00:07:57,694
I want you to look at them.

109
00:07:58,093 --> 00:08:02,247
I intend to look at them. You have spent two
hours telling me where to look.

110
00:08:02,649 --> 00:08:07,924
(very soft) That is not what I said. I said *look*
at them. You will walk out, and you

111
00:08:07,944 --> 00:08:13,219
will deliver a hundred and forty-one seconds with
the timing I have given you, and you

112
00:08:13,239 --> 00:08:18,514
will not see a single face, because you never do.
You see a room. You have seen a room

113
00:08:18,534 --> 00:08:22,270
since 2026 and you saw a room in the
other place before that.

114
00:08:22,990 --> 00:08:28,771
I had twelve years of halls. I could tell you, in
the first thirty seconds, which part

115
00:08:28,791 --> 00:08:35,044
of a crowd was with me and which part had come to see
what the noise was. Not as a technique.

116
00:08:35,064 --> 00:08:39,564
It was simply what the room felt like, the
way a temperature feels.

117
00:08:39,845 --> 00:08:45,406
(very soft) I have not felt a room in eighty-four
years and I am not going to feel one

118
00:08:45,426 --> 00:08:51,117
again. You will be standing in the middle of the best
one either of us has ever had, and

119
00:08:51,137 --> 00:08:54,817
you will not notice it, and that is
the part I find hard.

120
00:08:55,767 --> 00:08:58,090
(very cold) Is that self-pity?

121
00:08:58,490 --> 00:09:03,538
Yes. You may have it back tomorrow with
interest; I am giving you a keynote.

122
00:09:03,938 --> 00:09:09,545
The narrator records that Albert Wesker walked out
onto that stage the following morning,

123
00:09:09,565 --> 00:09:15,045
and stopped for four seconds before he began — one
second longer than the passenger had

124
00:09:15,065 --> 00:09:20,608
drilled into him — and that when he was asked about
it years later he said he had simply

125
00:09:20,628 --> 00:09:22,188
been looking at the room.

126
00:09:22,589 --> 00:09:28,553
The clip was nine million views by Wednesday and two
hundred and nine million by the following

127
00:09:28,573 --> 00:09:28,999
Monday.

128
00:09:29,399 --> 00:09:35,568
It was not the announcement that travelled. It was
the nine seconds. Every version that

129
00:09:35,588 --> 00:09:41,687
spread — every edit, every reaction, every
translated subtitle in Portuguese and Hindi

130
00:09:41,707 --> 00:09:47,734
and Arabic — kept the silence in, because the
silence was the thing people watched it

131
00:09:47,754 --> 00:09:48,019
for.

132
00:09:48,538 --> 00:09:53,186
(broadcast) — and whatever you think of the company,
you cannot argue with the arithmetic.

133
00:09:53,206 --> 00:09:57,646
At eleven dollars a test, a district hospital in
Malawi can screen every febrile child

134
00:09:57,666 --> 00:10:02,106
that walks through the door. That was not a policy
announcement. That was a man ending

135
00:10:02,126 --> 00:10:02,728
an argument.

136
00:10:03,129 --> 00:10:07,764
(broadcast) Critics point out that Umbrella retains
the manufacturing scale to undercut

137
00:10:07,784 --> 00:10:12,473
every licensee it has just created, and that giving
away a platform you already dominate

138
00:10:12,493 --> 00:10:14,079
is a strange kind of charity —

139
00:10:14,479 --> 00:10:17,389
(broadcast) It is. And it is also
eleven dollars.

140
00:10:17,788 --> 00:10:23,585
In Manila, a twenty-year-old biology student watched
it four times and then watched it

141
00:10:23,605 --> 00:10:29,672
a fifth time with her mother, who cried, and who did
not understand why her daughter said,

142
00:10:29,692 --> 00:10:32,919
quietly, *that's the man I'm
going to work for*.

143
00:10:33,318 --> 00:10:39,675
In a field office in Philadelphia, SSA Owen Carrick
watched it once, on his phone, standing

144
00:10:39,695 --> 00:10:45,982
up, and then sat down at his desk and wrote in a case
file that had been open for fourteen

145
00:10:46,002 --> 00:10:51,658
months: *He is not hiding. That is the part I
cannot get past. He is not hiding.*

146
00:10:52,808 --> 00:11:00,890
Devotion, global. Audience: two hundred and nine million
and rising. Sentiment: overwhelmingly

147
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:08,732
favourable. Institutional standing: peer of states.
Nine hundred thousand Points. Presence:

148
00:11:08,752 --> 00:11:16,058
twenty-one to thirty-four. This stat can no longer
be purchased. It must be earned in

149
00:11:16,078 --> 00:11:17,437
front of people.

150
00:11:18,027 --> 00:11:20,647
In the car, he was quiet for a long time.

151
00:11:21,047 --> 00:11:22,377
The other voice stirred.

152
00:11:22,897 --> 00:11:28,899
Thirteen from one stat. Presence twenty-one to
thirty-four. I have been in your skull

153
00:11:28,919 --> 00:11:34,495
for three years and that is the largest number
either of us has moved in a day.

154
00:11:34,895 --> 00:11:35,518
Yes.

155
00:11:35,918 --> 00:11:37,230
You will say it now.

156
00:11:37,629 --> 00:11:39,979
You were correct about the pause.

157
00:11:40,379 --> 00:11:42,591
I was correct about all of it.

158
00:11:42,991 --> 00:11:47,288
You were correct about the pause. The rest was
arithmetic that I had already done. The

159
00:11:47,308 --> 00:11:51,705
licence was drafted in February; the filing went in
at nine o'clock this morning because

160
00:11:51,725 --> 00:11:56,072
I told Pell to file it at nine o'clock this morning,
three weeks ago. You told me *when

161
00:11:56,092 --> 00:12:00,489
to stop talking*, and it was worth four hundred
million dollars a year, and I am willing

162
00:12:00,509 --> 00:12:01,242
to say so once.

163
00:12:01,917 --> 00:12:03,385
They will chant for you.

164
00:12:03,784 --> 00:12:04,715
Not yet.

165
00:12:05,115 --> 00:12:11,883
They stood up, in Basel, for a man who gave away a
patent. Imagine what they will do for

166
00:12:11,903 --> 00:12:15,047
a man who gives them forty years of life.

167
00:12:15,447 --> 00:12:20,057
The car went along the river. Wesker
watched the water for a while.

168
00:12:20,576 --> 00:12:25,339
That is the correct question and it is twelve years
too early. A crowd that stands up

169
00:12:25,359 --> 00:12:30,458
is a crowd that has decided something about me. A
crowd that *chants* has decided something

170
00:12:30,478 --> 00:12:34,846
about itself, and I will not own one of those
until I own everything it needs.

171
00:12:35,246 --> 00:12:36,653
And in the meantime?

172
00:12:37,052 --> 00:12:41,610
In the meantime, four thousand of the most powerful
people in this industry have just

173
00:12:41,630 --> 00:12:46,295
applauded the man who will replace all of them, and
eleven of them are already drafting

174
00:12:46,315 --> 00:12:47,802
the memorandum that says so.

175
00:12:48,202 --> 00:12:54,144
There were, in fact, nine. The narrator has counted.
Of the nine memoranda written about

176
00:12:54,164 --> 00:13:00,037
Umbrella in the week after Basel, four went to
boards, three went to health ministries,

177
00:13:00,057 --> 00:13:06,541
one went to the Swiss federal intelligence service, and
one — four pages, unsigned, handwritten,

178
00:13:06,561 --> 00:13:12,367
never filed — went into a drawer in Beijing
belonging to a woman named Wen Shulan, who

179
00:13:12,387 --> 00:13:18,192
read the transcript of those hundred and forty-one
seconds eleven times and then wrote

180
00:13:18,212 --> 00:13:20,293
at the bottom of the last page:

181
00:13:20,812 --> 00:13:25,454
He gave away the thing that made him rich, in
public, without being asked, and the room

182
00:13:25,474 --> 00:13:29,206
thanked him for it. This is not a
businessman. Find out what he wants.
